s a random book and reading that first  sentence does it draw me in why do I  call that narrative urgency well does it  create urgency does it make me want to  read the next sentence if so I keep  reading if not I stop in fact common  practice of mine is if a book but I  don't get past page fifty in a book  usually unless it's a masterpiece  it's because I don't want to waste my  time finishing a book just for the sake  of finishing now I realize that some  books get really good after page 180  they're American Psycho is a really  great example of that  I remember just sort of drudging through  it and it was you know a phenomenal book  for me in my early 20s it made me  realize you could do satire in a way  that didn't seem like conventional  satire but but anyway for the most part  I do not make it past page 50 unless  there is something really special going  on because every sentence needs a serve  of the neck so when we talked earlier  about I'll kill your darlings let's make  it a little more graphic kill your  babies these are things that you've  created you've birthed these words onto  the page but guess what don't get too  tied to it be willing to let go of  everything I know I know how beautiful  that sentence is that you that you  crafted I realize how madly in love you  are without alliteration that clever  twist of phrase that really cool  punctuation that that line that  character said that's all great but if  it doesn't serve a purpose in the  narrative you have to be willing to let  it go I know that's hard and so the  leading is important and you can create  a new a new draft if you want and that  way you have your zero draft that's just  sitting there elsewhere and you you  still have that composition should you  choose you want to go back to it you  could recess or to resuscitate your  babies I suppose but you know what  having that zero draft there as a backup  is fine then it'll allow you to delete  delete delete delete and then keep  deleting until you've gotten down to the  most urgent thing that is going to allow  your first sentence to take the reader  to the second sins it's going to propel  them to the third  and it's going to propel them all the  way to the end of your blog posts all  the way to the end of your novel your  memoir your nonfiction book your tweet  your email whatever it may be it's going  to get the reader to the end of that in  a way where they stay interested they  stay emotionally and intellectually  engaged that narrative urgency will  allow them to do that  my next step for you is to avoid too  many adverbs this is a sure sign of  amateur writing the overuse of adverbs  especially ly adverbs a woman in a story  isn't incredibly pretty she's beautiful  the sky isn't very blue it's as your  find the right words and that will allow  you to avoid using adverbs as crutches  and I think the reason that we do this  in writing so often is that it's how we  we do in our everyday vernacular we'll  say it's incredibly difficult it's just  difficult right you don't have to say  it's extremely this or incredibly that  or whatever avoiding these adverbs will  make your writing tighter and more  precise and and so again the best way to  do that as you find any ly adverb in  your writing delete it and then reread  the sentence aloud that's really  important in fact in in Missoula and  back in Dayton Ohio I'm often known as  the guy who looks somewhat schizophrenic  because I write aloud when I'm walking  to try to get pace and Anand diction  down appropriately and so you have all  of these you have tone you have timbre  you your writing takes a shape that your  author's voice takes a shape and so  reading it aloud is really important and  then delete and reread the thing aloud  and does that make the writing more  powerful often yes in fact yes for a few  reasons one is because you've eliminated  the excess and as a metal minimalism is  about and oh by the way the other  adverbs that stay in because they really  do make sense  to be there they're more powerful they  now hold more weight and that makes your  writing better my next tip for you is  follow the rules and then unfollow the  rules you should learn the rules so you  can break them effectively so I'm gonna  recommend a couple books at the end of  this the the podcast here because I  think it's important to not get too tied  up in the rules but know what the rules  are so you that you can you can break  them because otherwise your writing is  going to look haphazard your your  composition will will feel stilted  because you don't know what rules you're  breaking and which rules you are not and  if you are breaking the rules you're not  going to know why you're doing it and so  understanding what what the rules of  basic grammar are sentence structure etc  that's all that's all great but then you  can learn how to split infinitives and  you can learn how to have run-on  sentences you can learn how to have  sentence fragments these things work  very effectively in certain contexts as  long as you know why you're using them  and so you can learn about more advanced  grammatical rules as well or tactics  like polysyndeton or a cinder ton which  i won't i won't go into detail with  right now but it's these little these  little tools that will help you improve  your writing and a little bit more  precision and more brevity but also add  more more life to your writing by by  being willing to change those rules over  time my last tip for you is to read more  about writing now I don't like writing  about writing I think writing about  writing is like kissing your beautiful  sister Oh know what what I what I do  want to do though is I want to improve  my writing I really enjoy writing I very  rarely write about writing in fact  talking about writing this will likely  be the only writing podcast that I do  but you should  learn more about writing and part of  that has to do with reading about  writing so a coupl