es plenty of the rules and if  you're like me at first it'll drive you  crazy because she isn't very  prescriptive and I want a prescription  here is the definitive rule well let's  think about that for a minute language  has invented by humans and changes  dramatically over time  Shakespeare was supposedly modern  English and it is radically different  from how we write and talk and  communicate today and so language is  fluid it is ever-changing and so she  presents a great snapshot a picture of  the best standard written English but  also allows for deviations from that and  so grammatically correct we'll put a  link to that in the show notes and the  other book is gardeners modern usage  dictionary now it's not a traditional  dictionary where you look up words but  it is a usage dictionary and a Brian  Gardner is a grammarian and a  lexicographer and he does a phenomenal  job also not being too prescriptive but  but providing us with turns of phrases  and rules very specific rules around  usage it's a very big book if you were  to throw it down and your doorstop is  gonna sound like a bomb goes off but it  is a very useful reference and so I keep  those two books fairly close to me  they're on my bookshelf they're often  referenced because they help me be a  better writer especially when I'm  thinking about a rule is it different  than or different from or different - oh  yeah that's right it's different from  unless you're in Great Britain or  Australia different - works there as  well but in everyday vernacular we may  say different than this cup is different  than that cup but that's not correct in  good writing and so you'll be able to  figure out those little usage quirks and  many many many of them as a reference  guide in Gardner's book I think he does  a phenomenal job it's the best usage  dictionary that I've ever seen all right  real quick I'd love to move on to right  here right now this is some news updates  from the minimalists as you probably  know